#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078233 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/id #uuid "25a78c7c-e5c0-5048-b12c-6ed4848f5f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-438" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_2^x \\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\\,\\textrm d t$ for $x>2$." :question/worked-solution "The function\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\n$$\n\nis continuous on $[2,x]$, where $x>2$. So FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}∫_{2}^{x}\\frac{t^{3}}{t - 1}dt = \\frac{x^{3}}{x - 1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078234 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "b5e8576b-cd5c-5153-a492-1e8367456744" :question/math-academy-id "e-439" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t$." :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t =-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t & \\, \\, = \\, \\, \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right) \\\\ & \\, \\, = \\, \\, -\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right) \\\\ &= \\, \\, -\\dfrac{x^3}{x^2 +1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078235 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "03bb67e2-fc45-591c-beaf-890929fa0e11" :question/math-academy-id "e-440" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_2^{x^2}\\arctan t\\,\\textrm d t$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $f'(x)$, we first make the substitution $u=x^2$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}∫_{2}^{x^{2}}\\arctan tdt \\\\\n&= \\frac{du}{dx} \\cdot \\frac{d}{du}∫_{2}^{u}\\arctan tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous for all $t$, so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{du}{dx} \\cdot \\arctan (u) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\cdot \\arctan (u) \\\\\n&= 2x\\arctan (x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078236 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "bda18914-8a35-5d61-9b6d-d2747035e80b" :question/math-academy-id "e-441" :question/problem "Find $f'(1)$ if $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int_x^{x^2} (t^2 + 2t) \\,\\, \\textrm d t$." :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{x}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt &= ∫_{x}^{a}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + ∫_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt \\\\\n&= - ∫_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + ∫_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $x^2$.\n\nThe integrand $t^2 + 2t$ is continuous for all $t$, so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(- ∫_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + ∫_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{d}{dx}(∫_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) + \\frac{d}{dx}(∫_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} + 2x) + \\frac{du}{dx}\\frac{d}{du}(∫_{a}^{u}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x(u^{2} + 2u) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x((x^{2})^{2} + 2x^{2}) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x(x^{4} + 2x^{2}) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x^{5} + 4x^{3} \\\\\n&= 2x^{5} + 4x^{3} - x^{2} - 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=x^2$, as before.\n\nFinally, we can compute $f'(1)$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(1) &= 2(1)^{5} + 4(1)^{3} - (1)^{2} - 2(1) \\\\\n&= 2 + 4 - 1 - 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08dad672-a923-542a-b2a4-8075d5a00c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294485} {:answer/id #uuid "b87a0cd5-ed42-5831-8b73-6ef14d1fe940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294486} {:answer/id #uuid "622002d4-df72-5060-b2ac-6b231db87adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294487} {:answer/id #uuid "e352e279-23cc-5fd6-a6f9-5c667b866ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294488} {:answer/id #uuid "87f994a6-39cb-592b-ad83-8b7228910a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08dad672-a923-542a-b2a4-8075d5a00c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294485} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb530fa1-c008-55fc-925b-31d688efd41d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e902d8d9-f9a5-5a6b-9ee4-5e36cb9511d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-117278" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{a}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{x}^{3}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand ${t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-3{x}^{2}){u}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{(-{x}^{3})}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{x}^{3})+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{x}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{1}^{3}+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e52d69-8841-5a40-ac03-0698dcc64b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\cos x}{x^{2} + 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076360} {:answer/id #uuid "096683ff-5124-5438-bd0b-693462ab80b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sin x}{x^{2}} - \\frac{\\sin 3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076361} {:answer/id #uuid "375135a4-a4c7-59ab-8a5c-38cbfbf7f8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2} + 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076362} {:answer/id #uuid "69b43c13-0fef-5753-90c6-67be9bed5aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2} + 4} - \\frac{\\cos 3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076363} {:answer/id #uuid "7db5dcfc-d1c0-505a-9de0-9189c2ed20bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2}} + \\frac{\\cos 3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "375135a4-a4c7-59ab-8a5c-38cbfbf7f8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2} + 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076362} :answer-field/id #uuid "e033e820-ed37-5fc2-988c-a05e29446d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076359}] :question/id #uuid "0138eec2-eff9-517a-845d-ed8e6a92a2fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-4552" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{d}{dx}∫_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\cos t}{t^{2} + 4}dt$ for $x > 3$."}]}
